# Git Branching

A **branch** allows you to work on new features without affecting the main branch.

## Create a New Branch

git branch branch-name

## Switch to Another Branch

git checkout branch-name

Or:

git switch branch-name

## Create and Switch to a New Branch

git checkout -b branch-name

Or:

git switch -c branch-name

## Check Available Branches

git branch

The current branch is marked with:

*

---

# Merging Branches

To merge changes from another branch, first switch to the branch where you want the changes.

Example:

git checkout main

Then merge the branch:

git merge branch-name

Example:

git checkout main

git merge feature-branch

---

# Fast-Forward Merge

A **fast-forward merge** happens when the main branch has no new commits after the feature branch was created.

Git simply moves the branch pointer forward.

Example:

main

A --- B

feature

A --- B --- C --- D

After merging:

main

A --- B --- C --- D

---

# Three-Way Merge

A **three-way merge** happens when both branches have new commits.

Git creates a new merge commit to combine the changes.

Example:

        C --- D
       /         \
A --- B           M
       \         /
        E --- F

Here, `M` is the new merge commit.

